            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Alexander Hamilton</strong><br>\nAlexander Hamilton was born in 1755 on the island of Nevis, in the Caribbean. His early life was marked by hardship, including abandonment by his father and the death of his mother. Despite these challenges, he showed a keen intellect from a young age. His talents were recognized by local businessmen who funded his education in New York.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education and the American Revolution</strong><br>\nHamilton's education at King's College (now Columbia University) was interrupted by the American Revolution. He joined a New York volunteer militia in 1775 and soon became an artillery officer. His leadership and bravery caught the attention of General George Washington, who made him an aide-de-camp.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contributions to the Revolutionary War</strong><br>\nAs an aide, Hamilton was responsible for drafting correspondence on behalf of Washington. He longed for active military service and eventually got an opportunity to lead a battalion in the Battle of Yorktown in 1781. This decisive battle led to the surrender of the British General Cornwallis, marking a turning point in the war.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Lawyer and Politician</strong><br>\nAfter the war, Hamilton pursued a law career and quickly emerged as a powerful political figure. He was a delegate to the Congress of the Confederation and was instrumental in drafting the United States Constitution. As a leader of the Federalist Party, he advocated for a strong central government.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Federalist Papers</strong><br>\nHamilton co-authored the Federalist Papers, a series of essays promoting the ratification of the Constitution. Writing under the pseudonym \"Publius,\" he collaborated with James Madison and John Jay. These papers are considered fundamental to US constitutional interpretation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Secretary of the Treasury</strong><br>\nAs the first Secretary of the Treasury, Hamilton established the nation's financial system. His policies included the federal government assuming state debts, creating a national bank, and fostering friendly trade relations with Britain. His financial plans set the foundation for American prosperity.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
