          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Reynolds Affair</strong><br>\nHamilton's personal life was marked by scandal when he had an affair with Maria Reynolds. When accused of financial misconduct, he published the Reynolds Pamphlet, admitting to the affair but denying corruption. This incident tarnished his public image but proved his commitment to personal honor.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Rivalries and the Duel</strong><br>\nHamilton's outspoken nature led to fierce political rivalries, notably with Thomas Jefferson and Aaron Burr. His rivalry with Burr escalated to the point that, after a series of disputes, Burr challenged Hamilton to a duel in 1804. Hamilton was mortally wounded and died as a result of the duel.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Memory</strong><br>\nAlexander Hamilton's impact on the formation of the United States is profound. His vision of a robust federal government and a dynamic financial system has influenced American politics and economics for centuries. Today, he is remembered as a visionary statesman and a key architect of modern America.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
